      "headline": "Rewriting the Past: Why We Must Wonder What Almost Happened",
      "keyInsight": "The most dangerous illusion in human thinking is the feeling that history was completely inevitable. Once a war breaks out, a market crashes, or a leader wins, our brain instantly stitches together a neat story.\n\nWe tell ourselves that things simply had to happen that way. This trick gives us false comfort, but it ruins our foresight.\n\nThink of history like a wild river branching into hundreds of unseen channels. A single stray pebble could have redirected the entire flow.\n\nFlexible thinkers love inspecting those imaginary branches. They actively search for hidden scenarios where a tiny tweak would have flipped the outcome.\n\nRigid thinkers hate this exercise because it threatens their grand rules. If you cannot imagine how yesterday might have unfolded differently, you have zero chance of spotting tomorrow.",
      "content": "Picture a runaway train that narrowly misses an open drawbridge by two seconds. After the train races safely past, a casual spectator might say the train was always destined to arrive. But the mechanic who knows the rusted switch knows how close disaster was.\n\nThis thought experiment is what historians call a counterfactual, or an 'almost was' story. It means looking back at yesterday and asking what tiny spark could have lit an entirely different fire.\n\nRigid thinkers strongly dislike this mental game whenever it threatens their pet theories. When their preferred political outcome occurs, they declare it was an iron law of nature. They refuse to admit that pure luck or a random rainstorm played a part.\n\nYet, when their prediction fails, they suddenly invent wild alternate histories to excuse the miss. They argue that their forecast was practically right, and history was just hijacked by an unfair fluke.\n\nFoxes play a far fairer and more dangerous game with their own egos. They happily imagine alternate worlds where their own successes were pure lucky accidents. They ask themselves: 'If that speech was five minutes shorter, would the bill have died?' By testing how easily the past could have shattered, foxes stay acutely aware of random chance.\n\nThey know that life rarely runs on straight railroad tracks.\n\nWhen we practice imagining what almost happened, we shake off our hindsight arrogance. We realize that the world is messy, dynamic, and full of hidden forks in the road. Accepting that the past was fragile helps us spot the fragile turning points of the future.",
